proving mathematical statements:

proposition:
    is a true statement that has been proved to be true
theorem:
    is a particularly significant proposition
lemma:
    is a subsidrary proposition used in the proof of a theorem
corollary:
    is a proposition that follows almost immediately from a theorem

3.1 proving universally quantified statement

    prove method: To prove the universally quantified statement ∀ x ∈ S, P(x)
        1. choose a representative object x ∈ S, x is a placeholder
        2. show that the open stence P must be true for x, using known facts about the elements of S

    note: for proving universally quantified statement, if unable to find a single line of reasoning that works for all elements in the domain, then might consider a case analsysis

    disprove method: to disprove the universally quantified statement ∀ x ∈ S, P(x)
        find an element x ∈ S for which the open sentence P(x) is false. This process is called finding a counter-example.

3.2 proving existentially quantified statement

    proof method: To disprove the existentially quantified statement “∃ x ∈ S, P (x)”:
        Prove the universally quantified statement “∀ x ∈ S, ¬P (x)”.

3.3 proving implication
